(*
cells-sketch.ns — the PID node, written BEFORE the infrastructure exists.

Readme-driven, exactly as `demo-node.ns` was: none of this compiles today. `peek:`,
`peek:ifAbsent:`, `put:value:`, `clear:` and `changedSince:` DO NOT EXIST on Channel.
The point of the file is to see what the code looks like, and to let the diff between
option A and option C be the argument instead of a slide.

THE SCENARIO (David's third app, 2026-07-13). A PID controller. Always on, controlling
a process. Every 10 s it wakes its radio and checks its inbox for a setpoint/mode
change. It is fire-and-forget in both directions: nothing blocks on a request. And —
this is the part that decides the design — IT CRASHES, routinely, and the supervisor
restarts it, because that is the doctrine of this node (Milestone D).

  operator ──▶ porta ──▶ (radio) ──▶ bridge ──▶ ??? ──▶ PID job
                                                 ▲
                                    the whole question is what sits here

WHAT THE SPACE DOES TODAY (verified in src/space.c, 2026-07-13 — not assumed):
  · every name is a QUEUE. post appends; take removes; whenever: registers a reaction.
  · `space_dequeue` snapshots the channel's reaction list AT DRAIN TIME and then marks
    the event dead unconditionally (space.c ~262: `queue[idx].live = 0`). So an event
    posted while a job was down is DRAINED AND DROPPED. A restarted job that
    re-registers `whenever:` is NOT replayed anything. The stream does not wait.
  · there is no request/reply. A job cannot ASK another job for a value.

Those three facts together are why a restarted PID cannot recover its setpoint from
anything that exists now.

  A — the pigeonhole is in the space   (space.c gains a value slot + seq + tombstone)
  C — the pigeonhole is in the bridge  (an nsl Map inside the mailboy; no VM change)

Both are written out below, same scenario, same app, so the cost is visible rather
than argued.
*)
class CellsSketch packageUsing: manifest = () (

(* ======================================================================
   OPTION A — the pigeonhole is in the space.

   New Channel verbs (4 prims behind them: spacePeek / spacePut / spaceClear /
   spaceChangedSince). `put:value:` makes a name a cell; `out:value:` makes it a
   stream; using the wrong verb on an existing name is an error, exactly as read()
   on a directory is EISDIR. The expected-miss convention is the kernel's existing
   one (errors.md): `peek:ifAbsent:`, never a nil sentinel — which is also how the
   nsl surface sidesteps tuvm#21 while C still has to represent three states inside.
   ====================================================================== *)

  class PidJobA = ( | goal obs tel timers plant
                      sp   ::= nil.
                      mode ::= nil. | ) (

    (* start is ALSO restart: the supervisor re-runs this same thunk after a crash.
       Nothing here knows whether it is the first boot or the fifth restart — and
       nothing here mentions the network, the bridge, or porta. *)
    public start = (
      goal whenever: 'sp'   do: [ :v | self adopt: v ].    (* fires ON CHANGE *)
      goal whenever: 'mode' do: [ :v | self adoptMode: v ].
      self resume.
      ^ self )

    (* THE LINE THE WHOLE ARGUMENT IS ABOUT.
       The office burned down; the PA is new; the desk is empty. He walks to the
       pigeonhole and reads what is in it. No network. No bridge. No asking anyone. *)
    resume = (
      sp::   goal peek: 'sp'   ifAbsent: [ self safeSetpoint ].
      mode:: goal peek: 'mode' ifAbsent: [ #hold ].
      tel out: 'log' value: (Record tag: #log with: #text -> 'pid resumed'). )

    adopt: v     = ( sp:: v.   ^ self )
    adoptMode: v = ( mode:: v. ^ self )
    safeSetpoint = ( ^ 0 )                     (* fail safe, not fail arbitrary *)

    (* the control loop — unremarkable, and that is the point *)
    public tick = (
      | pv u |
      mode = #hold ifTrue: [ ^ self ].
      pv:: plant read.
      u::  self compute: pv toward: sp.
      plant drive: u.
      obs put: 'pv' value: pv.                 (* a cell: porta wants the LATEST, not all of them *)
      ^ self )

    compute: pv toward: target = ( ^ target - pv )     (* stand-in *)
  ) : (
    public goal: g obs: o tel: t timers: ti plant: p = (
      ^ self new setGoal: g obs: o tel: t timers: ti plant: p )
  )

  (* The mailboy, option A. He carries envelopes and reads only the address.
     `letter` is one decoded record off the radio: #path, #seq, #value, #dead.
     He never looks at #value — he cannot, and does not need to, tell a setpoint
     from a mode from an app manifest. *)
  class BridgeA = ( | radio goal obs tel sys cursor ::= 0. | ) (

    public start = (
      radio whenever: '' do: [ :dgram | self deliverAll: dgram ].
      ^ self )

    deliverAll: dgram = (
      (dgram at: #letters) do: [ :letter | self deliver: letter ].
      cursor:: dgram at: #seq. )

    deliver: letter = (
      | path |
      path:: letter at: #path.
      (letter at: #dead ifAbsent: [ false ])
        ifTrue:  [ goal clear: path ]                        (* un-deployed: a tombstone *)
        ifFalse: [ goal put: path value: (letter at: #value) ]. )

    (* Outbound: whatever changed since porta last acked, plus the queued letters.
       Again: he does not interpret. He asks the rack what moved. *)
    public checkIn = (
      | batch |
      batch:: obs changedSince: cursor.
      radio send: (Record tag: #push with: #obs -> batch
                                   with: #tel -> (tel drain)
                                   with: #sys -> (sys drain)).
      ^ self )
  ) : (
    public radio: r goal: g obs: o tel: t sys: s = (
      ^ self new setRadio: r goal: g obs: o tel: t sys: s )
  )

  (* Wiring, option A. Note what the supervisor hands the PID: a goal capability and
     an obs capability. Not a reference to the bridge. The PID cannot name the bridge,
     and has no idea a radio exists. *)
  wireA: space with: sup = (
    | bridge pid |
    bridge:: BridgeA radio: (self radioCap)
                     goal:  (space channel: 'goal')
                     obs:   (space channel: 'obs')
                     tel:   (space channel: 'tel')
                     sys:   (space channel: 'sys').
    pid:: PidJobA goal:   (space channel: 'goal')
                  obs:    (space channel: 'obs')
                  tel:    (space channel: 'tel')
                  timers: timers
                  plant:  (self plantCap).
    sup register: (space channel: 'pid') restart: [ pid start ] progress: (space channel: 'pid/ok').
    bridge start.
    pid start. )


(* ======================================================================
   OPTION C — the pigeonhole is in the mailboy's satchel.

   No VM change. The bridge holds path -> {value, seq, dead} in an nsl Map. Below is
   what the SAME scenario costs. Every added line is forced by one of the three facts
   at the top of this file, not by taste.
   ====================================================================== *)

  class PidJobC = ( | goal obs tel timers plant hello
                      sp   ::= nil.
                      mode ::= nil. | ) (

    public start = (
      goal whenever: 'sp'   do: [ :v | self adopt: v ].
      goal whenever: 'mode' do: [ :v | self adoptMode: v ].

      (* FORCED #1. There is nothing to peek. The letter that carried the setpoint was
         drained and dropped while this job was dead (space.c: the reaction snapshot is
         taken at drain time). So the job must ANNOUNCE ITSELF and beg for a re-send —
         a hand-rolled half of the request/reply the space does not have. *)
      hello out: '' value: (Record tag: #hello with: #want -> 'goal/sp'
                                               with: #and  -> 'goal/mode').

      (* FORCED #2. And it must run with NO setpoint until the mailboy gets around to
         answering. The window is unbounded: the bridge may itself be restarting. The
         PID is a control loop — it is driving a plant during this window. *)
      sp:: self safeSetpoint.
      mode:: #hold.
      ^ self )

    adopt: v     = ( sp:: v.   ^ self )
    adoptMode: v = ( mode:: v. ^ self )
    safeSetpoint = ( ^ 0 )

    public tick = ( (* identical to A *) ^ self )
  ) : ( )

  class BridgeC = ( | radio goal obs tel sys hello
                      cells ::= nil.        (* path -> Record{value, seq, dead} *)
                      cursor ::= 0. | ) (

    public start = (
      cells:: Map new.
      radio whenever: '' do: [ :dgram | self deliverAll: dgram ].

      (* FORCED #3. The mailboy now runs a lost-property office: he must listen for
         jobs announcing themselves and re-send. He is no longer stateless w.r.t. the
         node's jobs; he knows who exists and what they wanted. *)
      hello whenever: '' do: [ :req | self redeliver: req ].
      ^ self )

    deliver: letter = (
      | path |
      path:: letter at: #path.
      (* FORCED #4. He now keeps a private copy of everything he ever delivered.
         The satchel IS the node's desired state — and he is a supervised job, so his
         own routine crash empties it. Nothing else in the node has a copy. *)
      cells at: path put: letter.
      (letter at: #dead ifAbsent: [ false ])
        ifTrue:  [ goal out: path value: (Record tag: #withdrawn) ]   (* invent a sentinel: *)
        ifFalse: [ goal out: path value: (letter at: #value) ]. )
        (* ^ FORCED #5. There is no `clear:` on a stream, so "the operator un-deployed
             blink" has to be encoded as a magic value posted on the channel, and every
             consumer must know to recognise it. That sentinel is a protocol, spread
             across every job, with no compiler to check it. *)

    redeliver: req = (
      (* FORCED #6. Re-post the last letter for each prefix the requester named. It goes
         onto the SHARED channel, so every OTHER subscriber gets a duplicate too. Fine
         if applying a setpoint is idempotent; a real bug the first time someone puts a
         non-idempotent reaction on a goal path, and nothing prevents that. *)
      (req at: #want)  ifNotNil: [ :p | self repost: p ].
      (req at: #and)   ifNotNil: [ :p | self repost: p ]. )

    repost: path = (
      | held |
      held:: cells at: path ifAbsent: [ ^ self ].
      goal out: path value: (held at: #value). )

    public checkIn = (
      (* FORCED #7. `changedSince:` was a rack query. Now it is a scan of the satchel,
         written by hand, and the seq bookkeeping that the space would have done per
         path is the bridge's job. *)
      | batch |
      batch:: OrderedCollection new.
      cells keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v |
        (v at: #seq) > cursor ifTrue: [ batch add: v ] ].
      radio send: (Record tag: #push with: #obs -> batch freeze). )
  ) : ( )


(* ======================================================================
   WHAT THE DIFF SAYS

   The C column is not "some more nsl". Look at what each forced line actually is:

   #1 · the job begs for a re-send        -> a request/reply, hand-rolled, one-way
   #2 · it drives the plant with no sp    -> a REAL control hazard, unbounded window
   #3 · the bridge runs a lost-property   -> the mailboy now knows who works here
        office keyed by job
   #4 · the satchel holds the only copy   -> the one job whose ROUTINE crash (this is a
        of the node's desired state          let-it-crash node!) wipes node state
   #5 · a withdrawal is a magic value     -> tombstone-by-convention, unchecked, in every
        on a stream                          consumer
   #6 · re-post is a broadcast            -> duplicate delivery to uninvolved subscribers
   #7 · changedSince: becomes a hand       -> the seq/cursor bookkeeping moves out of the
        scan with hand-kept seq              one place that could do it uniformly

   Every one of those is the mailboy being asked to do something a mailboy does not do:
   remember, interpret, keep a directory of staff, and re-deliver on request. In option A
   he reads the address, drops the letter in the pigeonhole, and leaves — and the reason
   the PID survives its own crash is that THE RACK IS NOT IN HIS BAG.

   And note the one thing that does NOT appear in the A column: any mention of the
   network, the bridge, or porta, anywhere in PidJobA. That is the property the whole
   capability model is for.

   THE GATE THIS FILE PROPOSES (host only, no radio, no bridge):
     write a setpoint · crash the job · let the supervisor restart it · the job reads
     the correct setpoint back. That test passes under A and cannot be written under C.
   ====================================================================== *)
)
